page('/research/','Research and publications','''<p class="eyebrow">Academic work</p><h1>Research</h1><p class="lead">My doctoral research asks where human and robot information processing align, where they diverge, and how interaction can turn those differences into complementary capabilities.</p>'''+links([('Google Scholar',SCHOLAR)])+'''<section class="section"><h2>Doctoral thesis</h2><p>The thesis uses perception, cognition and response as a framework for investigating misalignment. It connects controlled visual-search experiments, mobile robot interfaces, an interactive chess system and a workshop on human–LLM collaboration.</p><p class="caption">TU Delft · Defence scheduled for 14 September 2026 · Selected thesis chapters</p>'''+links([(f'{i}. {label}',f'/assets/thesis-chapter-{i}.pdf') for i,(label,_) in enumerate(chapters,1) if i in (1,6)])+'''</section><section class="section"><h2>Publications &amp; manuscripts</h2>'''+pubhtml+'</section>','Research')
page('/about/','About','''<p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Renchi Zhang <span lang="zh">张任驰</span></h1><p class="lead">I am a PhD candidate in Cognitive Robotics at TU Delft, working on human–robot interaction, AI-driven robotic systems and evaluation.</p><p>I believe robots will increasingly become part of everyday human environments. My work starts by building systems people can actually encounter, then studying where perception, interpretation and action come apart.</p><p>During my PhD, that has meant integrating sensing, planning, manipulation and language on real robots, alongside experiments with human participants and multimodal models. I am now interested in moving further upstream: how embodied systems diagnose failures, choose informative experiments and improve their own behaviour.</p><h2>Background</h2><p>My PhD runs from July 2022 to September 2026, with my defence scheduled for 14 September 2026. My supervisors are Joost de Winter, Dimitra Dodou, Yke Bauke Eisma and Harleigh Seyffert.</p><p>I hold an MSc in Computer Science (Artificial Intelligence), cum laude, from Leiden University, and a BEng in Digital Media Technology from Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications. I also spent time at KU Leuven as a visiting student in surgical robotics.</p><h2>Current work</h2><p>Alongside my doctoral work, I have been exploring VLA post-training and offline robustness evaluation. I also contribute to evaluation datasets for human–AI hybrid systems through a part-time MARS V fellowship.</p>'''+links([('CV (PDF)','/assets/Renchi_Zhang_CV.pdf'),('Email','mailto:renchi.rz.zhang@gmail.com'),('GitHub',GITHUB),('LinkedIn','https://www.linkedin.com/in/renchizhang/'),('Google Scholar',SCHOLAR)]),'About',True)
page('/notes/','Notes and experiments','''<p class="eyebrow">Thinking in public</p><h1>Writing &amp; notes</h1><p class="lead">Draft essays, working hypotheses and proposed experiments connecting human–robot interaction with embodied intelligence.</p><section class="section">'''+notes+'</section>','Notes')

essay='''<p class="eyebrow">Essay draft · September 2026</p><h1>Why I still build robots for humans</h1><p class="lead">I believe that robots will live and work alongside us. During my PhD, I wanted to make that future concrete enough for people to encounter.</p><p>I began my doctorate in 2022. Over the following years, the capabilities of AI systems changed remarkably quickly. For someone coming from human–machine systems and human factors, it was easy to feel that every working prototype was already a generation behind.</p><p>But a newer model was only part of what I needed. I needed a robot that could see a board, move a piece, respond to a person, and keep the interaction going. I needed people to be able to walk alongside a robot and discover what felt natural, or confusing, about directing it. I needed students to see an AI-generated plan become a trajectory, with consequences they could inspect.</p><h2>Making an encounter possible</h2><p>Building a system for an experiment involves choices. A component may be less recent but easier to verify. A constrained task may reveal more than an impressive demonstration. An interface may need to make failure visible, even when hiding it would make the system look more capable.</p><p>OpenChessRobot gave me a way to study these choices in one complete system. Chess offered a state that could be checked, actions that could be executed, and explanations that could be compared with expert judgment. A fluent explanation did not always contain the strategic understanding it seemed to promise.</p><p>The visual-search study raised a related question at an earlier processing stage. Humans and models could agree on which conditions were difficult while producing very different response patterns. Similar outcomes were not sufficient evidence of similar perception.</p><h2>Following the question upstream</h2><p>These projects led me to think about interaction through perception, cognition and response. Where does the mismatch begin? What evidence would help a person detect it? Who should take over which part of the task?</p><p>I now want to ask more of these questions from the robot’s side. Could a system recognize that a failed task needs a new observation, a different plan, or a correction to execution? Could it propose a test that distinguishes these possibilities? Could the outcome change what it chooses to investigate next?</p><p>That is the connection I see between my work in HRI and my interest in embodied intelligence and scientific agents. The ambition is continuous: understand what it takes for intelligent machines to work alongside people, and build the mechanisms that make it possible.</p><p>My next experiment will start on a smaller, measurable problem: whether an agent can improve its experimental strategy. I want to evaluate the quality of its evidence, the experiments it repeats unnecessarily, and whether anything it learns carries over to a new task.</p><p>The robot still matters. It keeps the question attached to a world where an explanation, a decision and an action must eventually meet.</p><p class="caption">Draft for author review. Grounded in the supplied thesis Chapters 1–6 and the accompanying research statement.</p>'''
page('/notes/why-i-build-robots/','Why I still build robots for humans',essay,'Notes',True)
concept='''<p class="eyebrow">Concept note draft · Design hypothesis</p><h1>System 1, System 2, and embodied agents</h1><p class="lead">The useful question is what fast execution and slower diagnosis need to tell each other.</p><p>My thesis uses perception, cognition and response to locate difficulties in human–robot interaction. Those are processing functions. A fast/slow distinction asks a different question: how much deliberation does the system allocate, and when?</p><p>The two descriptions should not be treated as a one-to-one mapping. Perception can involve deliberate reinspection. A plan can be familiar and quickly retrieved. An action can require careful online correction. An end-to-end model can also fuse these functions without exposing separate modules.</p><h2>A proposed interface</h2><p>For an embodied agent, I would begin by making the information crossing between execution and diagnosis explicit.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Execution reports</th><th>Diagnosis returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Observation and estimated state</td><td>Alternative explanations to test</td></tr><tr><td>Expected versus observed outcome</td><td>A discriminating intervention</td></tr><tr><td>Action, timing and constraint violations</td><td>Success criteria and a stopping rule</td></tr><tr><td>Missing evidence or ambiguous state</td><td>What additional observation to request</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These records should refer to inspectable observations. A plausible narrative about a failure is a hypothesis until a test supports it.</p><h2>A small example</h2><p>Suppose a robot fails to pick up a chess piece. A board-localisation error, an incorrect piece estimate and a grasp-execution problem could produce a similar final outcome. Repeating the full task may provide little new information.</p><p>A better next experiment might acquire another view, verify the board transform, or test the grasp at a known position. The useful behaviour is the choice of intervention that separates candidate explanations.</p><h2>How I would test it</h2><p>I would compare a single controller with an explicit executor–diagnoser arrangement, keeping the underlying model, available evidence and total budget fixed. The evaluation would include diagnosis accuracy against controlled faults, unnecessary interventions, task recovery and cost.</p><p>This is a later experiment. First I want a reproducible scientific-agent loop and a trustworthy evaluator. Adding fast and slow components before establishing those would make the source of any improvement harder to identify.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>Status:</strong> an architectural hypothesis inspired by the thesis framework, not a finding about machine cognition or evidence that a robot should reproduce human cognitive architecture.</p></div><p class="caption">Source grounding: supplied thesis Chapter 1 (processing stages and modalities) and Chapter 6, Section 6.4 (limits of modular stage descriptions).</p>'''
page('/notes/fast-slow-embodied-agents/','System 1, System 2, and embodied agents',concept,'Notes',True)
proposal='''<p class="eyebrow">RSI-Scientist v0 · Proposed experiment</p><h1>Can an AI scientist improve its experimental strategy?</h1><p class="lead">Test whether structured memory improves evidence gathering, then test whether an updated strategy transfers to new tasks.</p><div class="callout"><p><strong>Status:</strong> design stage. No experiments have been run and no improvement is claimed.</p></div><h2>Research question</h2><p>Under the same model and resource limits, does a structured experiment ledger help an agent reach more reliable conclusions with fewer redundant experiments? Does a strategy extracted from that experience improve performance on unseen task families?</p><h2>Three initial conditions</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>What changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A · Fixed plan</td><td>The agent specifies its experiment sequence before seeing results. It may summarise the resulting evidence at the end.</td></tr><tr><td>B · Adaptive loop</td><td>The agent chooses subsequent experiments using prior results in an ordinary transcript.</td></tr><tr><td>C · Structured memory</td><td>The same adaptive loop records hypotheses, predictions, evidence, failed attempts and next-test rationales in an explicit ledger.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>All conditions receive the same tools, data access and budget. A versus B tests adaptivity; B versus C tests the memory package. Individual memory and reflection components would require later ablations.</p><h2>Evidence before implementation</h2><p>The candidate task is auditing encoded-hint influence in a human–AI complementarity dataset. Before using it, establish what the labels mean, preserve treatment/control pairing and hold out entire question families. A detectable hint does not establish that it caused a model’s answer.</p><p>A controlled task with planted, independently verified effects can calibrate the evaluator before any claims are made on the real dataset. Real-data conclusions without a reliable causal label should be scored as supported, contradicted or unresolved against the available evidence.</p><h2>What to measure</h2><p>The primary endpoint is the number of distinct, preregistered claims correctly resolved on held-out evidence at a fixed budget. Report false supported claims alongside it. Secondary measures cover redundant experiments, execution failures, appropriate abstention, latency, tokens and cost.</p><h2>The transfer test</h2><p>After development, freeze the proposed strategy update. Compare the updated policy with the original policy on unseen tasks, with fresh memory and equal budgets. Successful within-task iteration alone is insufficient evidence of strategy improvement.</p><h2>First pilot</h2><p>A provisional debugging pilot uses three independent task families, three random seeds, three conditions and at most eight experiment slots per run. This is a feasibility check, not a powered confirmatory study. Model identity and monetary limits must be fixed before live calls begin.</p><p>The immediate dependency is a repository, schema, representative records and ground-truth definition for the chosen task.</p>'''
page('/notes/research-loop/','Can an AI scientist improve its experimental strategy?',proposal,'Notes',True)
print('Generated',len(list(OUT.rglob('index.html'))),'pages')

